<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mansura, Sindh</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mansura was the capital of the Arab province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, founded in the 730s by the Umayyad governor Amr ibn Muhammad near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>, north-east of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. From the 850s it was the seat of the Habbari dynasty, and Arab geographers described it as a prosperous trading city. It declined after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a>'s conquest in the 1020s, and its ruins lie near Shahdadpur.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mansura-sindh.jpg" alt="Mansura, Sindh" width="284" height="276" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wasimawan at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coins_Amirs_of_sindh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
